Consider a planar Newtonian (free-molecular) aerodynamic model in which a compact body B ⊂ ℝ² with piecewise smooth boundary and uniform mass density moves through a medium of point particles initially at rest. Particles do not interact with each other; when a particle meets the moving boundary ∂B it undergoes an instantaneous elastic collision (in the body's instantaneous rest frame) and thereafter moves freely, while B evolves rigidly under the resultant force and torque from all such collisions. Given an initial position, orientation, linear velocity, and angular velocity of B, the resulting motion is the rigid-body trajectory (center-of-mass position and orientation as functions of t ≥ 0).

Excluding the trivial case in which B is a disc, describe explicitly the motion of B for all t ≥ 0 when B has one of the following simple shapes:
(a) an ellipse (with unequal semi-axes);
(b) a triangle;
(c) the rod B = [−1, 1] × {0}.

In each case, determine the qualitative and quantitative long-time behavior of the linear and angular velocities and of the trajectory of the center of mass.

